⚙️ Config Mapping – Simple Explanation

1. What is Config Mapping?

“The Config Mapping sheet is the control table of the macro.
It tells the system where to get the data and where to put the data.”

Instead of hardcoding cell addresses in VBA, we use the Config sheet to define:

Source sheet name

Source column or cell

Target column in the Summary sheet

Material type condition (Direct / Indirect)



---

2. How It Works

“When the macro runs:”

1. The system reads the Config Mapping sheet first.


2. It checks each row of the mapping table.


3. For each mapping:

It gets the value from the source file (based on sheet and cell/column).

It transfers the value to the correct column in the Summary sheet.



4. It repeats this process until all mapped fields are completed.



So basically:

👉 Config Sheet = Instruction
👉 Macro = Follows the instruction
👉 Summary Sheet = Final output


---

3. Why We Use Config Mapping

No need to edit VBA code if column changes

Easy to maintain

Flexible for new fields

Less risk of coding error


“If there are changes in format, we only update the Config sheet, not the macro.”


---

4. Simple Example You Can Say

“For example, if Supplier Name is in Sheet1 cell B5,
the Config Mapping tells the macro:

Read Sheet1

Get value from B5

Put it in column ‘Supplier Name’ in Summary


If next time the location changes, we only update the Config.”


